n 1510–1511, a humanist, writer and philosopher.", "newValue": "Adam of Łowicz (c. 1450 – February 17, 1514) was a prominent Polish polymath and academic administrator whose brief but impactful career significantly shaped the intellectual landscape of Kraków during the nascent Polish Renaissance. As a professor of medicine, philosopher, and twice-rector of the University of Krakow (Jagiellonian University) in 1510 and 1511, he stood at the critical juncture where late medieval scholasticism converged with the burgeoning humanism of Central Europe. His work helped to integrate classical medical traditions with new pedagogical and philosophical approaches, making him a key figure in the modernization of Polish higher education during the reign of King Sigismund I the Old.

